          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T16 or T9</h3>
          <p>
            Read the table for what is the same. Deck size, incline, speed, motor rating, folded length and
            warranty are identical. The $500 between their sale prices buys the 16-inch touchscreen with
            its built-in apps, and 25 lb more rated capacity. If the screen is what will get you on the
            machine four times a week, that is a fair price for it. If you would use a tablet on the media
            shelf anyway, the T9 is the better buy; our T9 review also notes that independent reviewers
            found its Bluetooth would not run a heart-rate strap and an app at once.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">T16 or T6</h3>
          <p>
            The T6 is $1,000 cheaper at the sale prices we saw, with a 20-inch deck, a 325 lb rating, a
            lower motor rating on the same test basis, and a shorter, flat fold. For walking and running at
            moderate pace by a user of average build it does the core job; the T16 adds width, capacity and
            the screen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Alternatives Outside Bowflex</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you want classes to control the machine:</strong> the T16’s apps do not change speed
            and incline for you. iFIT machines such as the
            <a href="/nordictrack-commercial-1750-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack Commercial 1750</a>
            do, with a 22 by 60-inch deck and decline, around a paid iFIT membership. Our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> covers whether that
            membership pays for itself.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you never want a screen subscription and can live without a built-in display:</strong>
            Horizon’s Studio Series sends data to your own apps with no platform of its own; the
            <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT</a>
            and the wider models in our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon roundup</a>
            are the comparison.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you need decline:</strong> the T16 has none; see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">decline roundup</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you weigh more than about 325 lb:</strong> the T16’s 375 lb rating gives more margin
            than most folding treadmills, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            covers machines rated higher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy the Bowflex T16</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if a big built-in screen is what will keep you training.</strong> Streaming,
            browsing and JRNY on a 16-inch display, with no required subscription, on a 22 by 60-inch deck
            with 15% incline, is the T16’s whole case.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if you are a heavier runner who also wants the screen.</strong> The 375 lb rating
            is the highest in Bowflex’s range, backed by lifetime frame and motor cover.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the T9 instead</strong> if a tablet on a shelf would do; it shares the deck, incline,
            motor rating and warranty for $500 less at the prices we saw.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plan for headphones</strong>, and check the fixed screen height against the tallest
            user if you can see one in a store.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it on sale.</strong> It was $1,899 against $2,299 regular when we checked, and
            Bowflex discounts often; compare the delivered price for the delivery option you need. Our
            <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand overview</a>
            covers the rest of the range and the 2024 change of ownership to Johnson Health Tech.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Bowflex T16 treadmill worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is if the 16-inch touchscreen is what will keep you training. It has a 22 by 60-inch deck, 0 to 15% incline, 12 mph, a 375 lb rating and lifetime frame and motor cover. The T9 shares the deck, incline, speed and motor rating for $500 less at the prices we saw, so without the screen it is hard to justify. We have not tested it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Bowflex T16 need a JRNY subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Bowflex says the T16 works without a JRNY subscription, and a free JRNY account unlocks some programs. Wi-Fi is needed only for the apps on the screen. Streaming apps use your own existing subscriptions, and full JRNY content needs a paid membership, whose price we do not quote because it changes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Bowflex T16 vs T9: what is the difference?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have a 22 by 60-inch deck, 0 to 15% incline, 12 mph, a 3.5 HP test rating, a 48.6-inch fold and the same warranty. The T16 adds a 16-inch touchscreen with built-in apps and a 375 lb rating against 350 lb. On 14 October 2026 the sale prices were $1,899 and $1,399.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor does the Bowflex T16 have?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bowflex rates it at 3.5 HP and footnotes that this is a measurement in a test setting that indicates robustness and does not reflect operational horsepower. It is the same rating as the T9, so it cannot be compared directly with another brand’s CHP figure. The motor carries a lifetime warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use Peloton or Zwift on the Bowflex T16?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, but not on its screen. Bowflex says to run Peloton or Zwift on a phone or tablet paired to the console over Bluetooth, which sends speed, incline and heart rate using FTMS. The apps read the data; set speed and incline yourself with the QuickDial controls.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Bowflex T16 fold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, on a pin with gas-shock assistance, but not flat. It goes from 77.2 inches long to 48.6 inches and stays 67.6 inches tall. Bowflex gives its weight as 288 lb in one place and 293.2 lb in another, so plan for a heavy machine that stays in one room.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Bowflex T16</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The T16 is a good machine that is easy to overpay for. These are the errors that cost buyers money or disappoint them later.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for the screen and then using a tablet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The T9 shares the T16’s deck, incline, speed, motor rating and warranty for $500 less at the prices we saw. If you would prop your own tablet on the shelf, the T16’s main advantage goes unused.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on the built-in speakers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Both independent reviews found the 5-watt speakers weak against the noise of a running belt. Budget for Bluetooth headphones if you plan to stream shows while running.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading 3.5 HP as a continuous rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bowflex says its 3.5 HP is a test-setting figure, not operational horsepower. Do not rank it against another brand’s 3.5 CHP, and remember the cheaper T9 carries the same rating.</p>
